    "setupNotes": [
      "Factorio 2.1.24 Space Age. 19 assembling machine 2 (no modules), red belts, fast/long-handed/bulk arms. WEST inputs, one labelled entrance each: iron plates, electronic circuits, copper plates, steel plates, stone bricks, plastic bars. EAST: eight passive provider chests in one column, one product each; each stops at its own cap, set in the constant combinator beside the fast-belt chest (edit one number). Gears, cable, red circuits, yellow belts, undergrounds, splitters, inserters and AM1 are made inside. Preserve the red ring wires, the green cap wires, all arm filters, the entrance belt conditions, the gear clock, the two-tile gear belt behind the drill, the fast-inserter reserve decider and the fast-inserter feed decider. Supply electricity at the marked substation.",
      "Feed each west entrance with its labelled material only; the harness feeds every entrance as a full red belt. Iron mostly goes to the rear belt behind the bottom row, which feeds three iron-gear-wheel machines and the underground-belt, assembling-machine-1 and transport-belt machines. The splitter, fast-inserter, inserter and electric-mining-drill machines take iron from the ring, which gets it through one admission arm fed by a bulk arm that lifts iron off the rear belt.",
      "Electronic circuits are a supplied input (84/min at the floors, more when one product is drawn alone); one green-circuits supplier covers this mall.",
      "Caps live in one constant combinator next to the fast-belt chest (one negative number per product). Each chest arm runs only while its product is below its cap.",
      "Fast inserters are also a bulk-inserter ingredient. The fast-inserter machine's inserter-feed arm runs while the fast-inserter chest is below its cap or the ring holds fewer than 4 fast inserters (one decider), so the bulk-inserter machine keeps getting fast inserters while that chest is full. A second decider lets the fast-inserter chest arm take only fast inserters above a ring reserve of 14.",
      "Gear arms of the five gear-hungry top-row finals take turns in a seven-window clock (7.5 s each, every window owned); the drill gets its gears straight from the gear machine beside it over a two-tile belt; yellow belts, inserters and AM1 take gears freely because their own ring limits bound them.",
      "Plan supply above the entrance panels if all eight products should hold their floors at once: the panels are recipe totals at the floors, and with everything drawn every product runs above its floor. Exploration measurements with the harness's supply cap (the whole protocol with all eight cap phases, not part of the qualification): the protocol passed with every entrance capped at 1.7, 1.75, 1.8, 1.9 and 2 times its panel figure, and failed at 1.6 times (fast inserters 5.9 per minute in recovery against 6). At exactly the panel figures with all eight drawn, the drill made 0 per minute, fast inserters 2.3, furnaces 0.8, bulk inserters 0.95 and fast belts 7.95; assembling machine 2, fast undergrounds and fast splitters held their floors. With full belts the entrances took about 201 electronic circuits, 732 iron, 38 steel, 26 stone brick, 30 plastic and 30 copper per minute. With less supply the mall still fills every chest in turn: fed exactly the panel figures with nothing collected, all eight chests reached their caps; only the all-eight-at-once floors are lost."
    ],
